-- Q1 [Chemistry of the Groups · easy · Skill 1 · ans A] (g41)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt41$Potassium reacts with chlorine to form an ionic compound. Because potassium belongs to group 1, the charge of the potassium ion in this compound is most likely$qt41$,
    $op41$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(+1\\), from losing its single \\(ns^1\\) valence electron"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+2\\), from losing both its outermost electrons"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(-1\\), from gaining one electron to complete its shell"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(+1\\), from gaining one proton in the nucleus"}]$op41$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex41$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. Group 1 (alkali) metals have the valence configuration \(ns^1\): a single electron outside a noble-gas core. Losing that one electron yields a cation with a stable noble-gas configuration, so potassium forms \(\text{K}^+\) with a \(+1\) charge. (Choice B) Removing a second electron requires breaking into the noble-gas core, which carries a prohibitively high second ionization energy; alkali metals do not reach \(+2\) under ordinary chemical conditions. (Choice C) Gaining an electron is characteristic of nonmetals such as the group 17 halogens, which need one electron to complete an \(np^5\) shell; an \(ns^1\) metal attains the noble-gas configuration by losing, not gaining, an electron. (Choice D) Ionic charge arises from transfer of electrons between atoms, not from adding a proton; altering the proton count would change the element's identity rather than form an ion. Skill 1$ex41$,
    'easy', '4E', $cc41$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc41$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a41B$Over-ionizing past the noble-gas core to reach +2$a41B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a41C$Treating a metal as if it gains electrons like a nonmetal$a41C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a41D$Confusing ion formation with changing proton (nuclear) count$a41D$ FROM q;

-- Q2 [Chemistry of the Groups · easy · Skill 1 · ans B] (g42)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt42$Chlorine sits in group 17 of the periodic table. When chlorine reacts with a metal to reach a noble-gas configuration, the charge of the monatomic ion it forms is most consistent with which valence electron count?$qt42$,
    $op42$[{"label":"A","text":"A \\(3s^2 3p^4\\) valence shell, so chlorine gains two electrons to form \\(\\text{Cl}^{2-}\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"A \\(3s^2 3p^5\\) valence shell, so chlorine gains one electron to form \\(\\text{Cl}^{-}\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"A \\(3s^2 3p^6\\) valence shell, so chlorine loses one electron to form \\(\\text{Cl}^{+}\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"A \\(3s^2 3p^5\\) valence shell, so chlorine loses five electrons to form \\(\\text{Cl}^{5+}\\)."}]$op42$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex42$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. The halogens occupy group 17, and their defining feature is an \(ns^2 np^5\) valence configuration that holds seven valence electrons—one short of a full octet. For chlorine this is \(3s^2 3p^5\). Reaching a noble-gas configuration requires gaining a single electron rather than removing electrons, which fills the \(3p\) subshell to \(3s^2 3p^6\) (the argon configuration) and produces the characteristic \(-1\) anion \(\text{Cl}^{-}\). (Choice A) A \(3s^2 3p^4\) shell would hold only six valence electrons, which is the configuration of group 16 (chalcogens such as sulfur); those elements are two electrons short of an octet and form \(2-\) anions, whereas chlorine has seven valence electrons and is only one short. (Choice C) A \(3s^2 3p^6\) shell already represents a completed octet, so this describes the product of electron gain, not the neutral atom; a neutral halogen does not have a full octet, and forming a \(\text{Cl}^{+}\) cation moves away from the noble-gas configuration rather than toward it. (Choice D) Although the \(3s^2 3p^5\) valence shell is correct, removing five electrons to leave a bare \(3s^0 3p^0\) configuration is far more endothermic than gaining a single electron; an electronegative group 17 element completes its octet by acquiring one electron, not by stripping its entire valence shell. Skill 1$ex42$,
    'easy', '4E', $cc42$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc42$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a42A$Assigns the group 16 (chalcogen) valence configuration and 2- charge to a group 17 element$a42A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a42C$Confuses the filled product configuration with the neutral atom and reverses the direction of electron transfer$a42C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a42D$Correct valence shell but treats the halogen as losing all valence electrons rather than gaining one$a42D$ FROM q;

-- Q3 [Chemistry of the Groups · easy · Skill 1 · ans C] (g43)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt43$A flask of argon, \(\text{Ar}\), shows almost no tendency to enter chemical reactions under ordinary conditions. Which feature of its valence electron arrangement most directly accounts for this lack of reactivity?$qt43$,
    $op43$[{"label":"A","text":"Its valence shell holds a single unpaired \\(ns^1\\) electron that resists pairing."},{"label":"B","text":"Its valence shell is exactly half filled, giving an especially stable configuration."},{"label":"C","text":"Its valence shell is completely filled with eight electrons, leaving no strong drive to gain, lose, or share electrons."},{"label":"D","text":"Its valence shell is entirely empty, so there are no electrons available to form bonds."}]$op43$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex43$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.' The noble gases of group 18 occupy the rightmost column of the periodic table, and each (apart from helium) carries a full complement of eight valence electrons in an \(ns^2 np^6\) configuration. A completely filled valence shell is energetically very stable, so the atom has essentially no driving force to gain, lose, or share electrons; this satisfied octet is the direct reason argon is chemically inert under ordinary conditions, making C correct. (Choice A) A lone \(ns^1\) valence electron is the configuration of the group 1 alkali metals, which are highly reactive because that single electron is readily lost; this describes a reactive arrangement, not the inert one of a noble gas. (Choice B) A half-filled valence shell offers only modest extra stability and still leaves room to gain or share electrons, so it does not produce the near-complete inertness seen in group 18; argon's valence shell is full, not half filled. (Choice D) An entirely empty valence shell is not the situation for a neutral noble gas, whose valence shell is full rather than vacant; an atom with no valence electrons would still be driven to acquire them, which is the opposite of the stability that makes argon unreactive. Skill 1$ex43$,
    'easy', '4E', $cc43$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc43$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a43A$alkali-metal ns1 configuration attributed to noble gas$a43A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a43B$half-filled-shell stability overextended to full inertness$a43B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a43D$inertness from empty valence shell rather than full octet$a43D$ FROM q;

-- Q4 [Chemistry of the Groups · easy · Skill 1 · ans D] (g44)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt44$A chemistry student is setting up a reaction between strontium metal and a halogen and needs to predict the formula of the product. Knowing only that strontium (\(\text{Sr}\)) sits in group 2 of the periodic table, what charge should the student expect the monatomic strontium ion to carry in the resulting compound?$qt44$,
    $op44$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Sr}^{+}\\), formed by losing a single one of its valence electrons."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Sr}^{2-}\\), formed by gaining two electrons the way a group-16 nonmetal does."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Sr}^{2+}\\), formed by gaining two electrons to match the configuration of the following noble gas."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Sr}^{2+}\\), formed by losing both of its valence \\(ns^2\\) electrons to match the preceding noble gas."}]$op44$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex44$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. Strontium is a group-2 (alkaline earth) metal, so its outermost shell holds two electrons in an \(ns^2\) configuration. To reach the stable closed-shell configuration of the preceding noble gas (krypton), strontium gives up both of these valence electrons, producing the cation \(\text{Sr}^{2+}\). The group number for the main-group metals directly predicts the maximum positive charge, so a group-2 metal forms a \(+2\) ion. (Choice A) Losing only one valence electron would yield \(\text{Sr}^{+}\), but this leaves a single \(ns^1\) electron in the outer shell and does not reach a noble-gas configuration; group-2 metals lose both \(ns^2\) electrons. (Choice B) A charge of \(2-\) corresponds to gaining two electrons, which is the behavior of a group-16 nonmetal that needs two electrons to complete its octet; a group-2 metal has low ionization energy and loses electrons rather than gaining them. (Choice C) The charge \(+2\) is correct, but a cation is not formed by gaining electrons, and the relevant target is the preceding noble gas (krypton) reached by electron loss, not a following noble gas reached by electron gain. Skill 1$ex44$,
    'easy', '4E', $cc44$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc44$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a44A$Removing only one valence electron instead of both, stopping the ionization one step early$a44A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a44B$Treating the alkaline earth metal as an electron acceptor like a group-16 nonmetal, reversing the direction of electron transfer$a44B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a44C$Correct magnitude of charge but attributing it to electron gain and the wrong (following) noble-gas target$a44C$ FROM q;

-- Q5 [Chemistry of the Groups · easy · Skill 1 · ans A] (g45)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt45$A sealed flask holds a sample of the elemental form of chlorine at room temperature, with no other element present. Which formula represents the species that predominates in the flask?$qt45$,
    $op45$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Cl}_2\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Cl}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Cl}^-\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Cl}_2^-\\)"}]$op45$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex45$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. In their uncombined elemental state, the halogens (Group 17) do not persist as isolated atoms; each atom pairs through a single covalent bond, so the free elements exist as the diatomic molecules \(\text{F}_2\), \(\text{Cl}_2\), \(\text{Br}_2\), and \(\text{I}_2\). A flask containing only elemental chlorine at room temperature therefore holds \(\text{Cl}_2\). (Choice B) A lone \(\text{Cl}\) atom carries an unpaired electron and is a highly reactive radical; it is not the stable predominant form of the free element, which pairs into \(\text{Cl}_2\). (Choice C) \(\text{Cl}^-\) is the chloride anion, formed only when chlorine gains an electron from another species; the prompt specifies elemental chlorine alone, which is neutral, not ionic. (Choice D) \(\text{Cl}_2^-\) carries a net negative charge and would require an added electron; neutral elemental chlorine has no such charge, so this is not the species present. Skill 1$ex45$,
    'easy', '4E', $cc45$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc45$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a45B$treating the free halogen as monatomic atoms rather than diatomic molecules$a45B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a45C$confusing the neutral free element with the chloride ion the halogen commonly forms$a45C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a45D$keeping the diatomic count but adding an unwarranted ionic charge$a45D$ FROM q;

-- Q6 [Chemistry of the Groups · medium · Skill 1 · ans B] (g46)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt46$Among the metals of period 4, potassium displaces hydrogen from water far more vigorously than calcium or any later metal in the row. Holding the comparison within the period, this heightened reactivity of \(\text{K}\) is best attributed to which combination of atomic properties?$qt46$,
    $op46$[{"label":"A","text":"The smallest atomic radius in the period paired with the lowest first ionization energy, so the valence electron sits closest to the nucleus yet leaves most readily."},{"label":"B","text":"The largest atomic radius in the period paired with the lowest first ionization energy, so the single \\(ns^1\\) electron is weakly held and lost with the least energy."},{"label":"C","text":"The largest atomic radius in the period paired with the highest electron affinity, so the atom most readily gains an electron to complete its shell."},{"label":"D","text":"A half-filled valence subshell paired with the lowest first ionization energy, so the configuration is stabilized while still surrendering an electron easily."}]$op46$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex46$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. Across a period, atomic radius is largest and first ionization energy is lowest at the far left, where the alkali metal sits. Potassium's valence configuration is \(\left[\text{Ar}\right]4s^1\): a single \(ns^1\) electron lies far from the nucleus and is poorly shielded against the core, so the weak nuclear hold combined with the large radius means very little energy is needed to remove it. Because metallic reactivity tracks the ease of losing valence electrons to form a cation (here \(\text{K}^+\)), the element with the largest radius and lowest first ionization energy in the period is the most reactive metal, which is why \(\text{K}\) outpaces \(\text{Ca}\) and the elements to its right. (Choice A) The alkali metal has the largest radius in its period, not the smallest; the smallest radius belongs near the right of the period, where the valence electron is held most tightly and ionization energy is high, the opposite of what favors reactivity. (Choice C) High reactivity of an alkali metal stems from readily losing an electron, not gaining one; electron affinity describes electron capture and is associated with nonmetal (anion-forming) behavior, not with a metal driven to form a cation. (Choice D) A half-filled subshell is a feature of certain mid-block configurations and confers extra stability that tends to raise ionization energy; the \(ns^1\) alkali metal has no such half-filled stabilization, and it is the absence of any added stability that leaves its lone valence electron easily removed. Skill 1$ex46$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc46$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc46$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a46A$Swaps the radius trend (smallest instead of largest) while keeping the correct ionization-energy claim, inverting the periodic radius relationship$a46A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a46C$Confuses metallic reactivity (electron loss) with electron affinity (electron gain), invoking the wrong driving property$a46C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a46D$Imports the half-filled-subshell stability fact from a different periodic context where it does not apply to an ns1 alkali metal$a46D$ FROM q;

-- Q7 [Chemistry of the Groups · medium · Skill 1 · ans C] (g47)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt47$A chemist mixes hydrogen gas with fluorine gas in a sealed flask, and the two combine according to \(\text{H}_2 + \text{F}_2 \rightarrow 2\,\text{HF}\). Given that both hydrogen and fluorine are nonmetals, which best accounts for the nature of the \(\text{HF}\) produced?$qt47$,
    $op47$[{"label":"A","text":"An ionic salt, because the more electronegative fluorine strips an electron away from hydrogen to form a lattice of \\(\\text{H}^+\\) and \\(\\text{F}^-\\) ions."},{"label":"B","text":"A metallic compound, in which the valence electrons of hydrogen and fluorine merge into a shared, delocalized electron sea."},{"label":"C","text":"A covalent (molecular) hydrogen halide \\(\\text{HX}\\), because hydrogen and a halogen are both nonmetals that share a bonding electron pair."},{"label":"D","text":"No compound at all, because halogens such as fluorine react only with metals and cannot combine with another nonmetal like hydrogen."}]$op47$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex47$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. Halogens react directly with hydrogen to form hydrogen halides of the form \(\text{HX}\). Because hydrogen and fluorine are both nonmetals, neither one readily gives up an electron to the other; instead they share a bonding electron pair, producing a covalent molecular compound. The resulting \(\text{HF}\) is a molecular gas (it dissolves in water to give an acidic solution), making choice C correct. (Choice A) Bonding between two nonmetals does not transfer an electron to build a lattice of separate ions; even though fluorine is highly electronegative, the shared pair in \(\text{HF}\) makes it a polar covalent molecule, not an ionic salt. (Choice B) A delocalized electron sea characterizes bonding among metal atoms; \(\text{HF}\) contains no metal and its electrons are localized in a shared bond between two specific atoms, so the description does not apply. (Choice D) Halogens are not restricted to reacting with metals; the direct combination of \(\text{H}_2\) and \(\text{F}_2\) to give \(\text{HF}\) is itself a reaction of a halogen with a nonmetal. Skill 1$ex47$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc47$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc47$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a47A$Treats the highly electronegative halogen as forcing a full electron transfer (ionic salt) instead of electron sharing, reversing the covalent sharing relationship between two nonmetals.$a47A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a47B$Imports the real concept of a delocalized electron sea (metallic bonding) and misapplies it to a nonmetal-nonmetal product.$a47B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a47D$Holds the false belief that halogens react exclusively with metals, denying that they combine directly with hydrogen.$a47D$ FROM q;

-- Q8 [Chemistry of the Groups · medium · Skill 1 · ans D] (g48)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt48$While building a periodic table for a chemistry display, a student notices that helium is printed at the top of group 18 above neon and the other noble gases, even though helium's only electrons are a \(1s^2\) pair and it never reaches the eight valence electrons shared by the rest of that column. Which of the following best accounts for grouping helium with the noble gases despite this apparent mismatch?$qt48$,
    $op48$[{"label":"A","text":"Helium belongs more naturally with group 2 because its \\(1s^2\\) configuration ends in an \\(s^2\\) outer subshell, matching the \\(ns^2\\) pattern of the alkaline earth metals."},{"label":"B","text":"Helium does in fact carry eight valence electrons like the other members of group 18, so it satisfies the same octet that makes the heavier noble gases inert."},{"label":"C","text":"Helium's placement in group 18 reflects only historical convention with no electronic justification, since its two electrons match no other noble gas."},{"label":"D","text":"Helium's \\(1s^2\\) valence shell is completely filled — the first shell (\\(n=1\\)) holds a maximum of only two electrons — so helium has a full outer shell (a filled duet) and the same chemical inertness as the other noble gases despite lacking eight valence electrons."}]$op48$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex48$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.' Group membership is defined by a shared valence-shell electronic structure that confers similar chemical behavior, not by a fixed count of eight valence electrons. The noble gases are grouped together because each has a completely filled outermost shell, which makes the atom chemically inert. For helium that outermost shell is the first shell, and the first shell (\(n=1\)) contains only the \(1s\) subshell, whose maximum capacity is two electrons. Helium's \(1s^2\) configuration therefore fills the \(n=1\) shell completely, giving a stable filled duet that leaves no low-energy vacancy and no easily available electron, exactly the closed-shell stability and resistance to reaction that characterize the heavier noble gases. The hinge is the \(n=1\) capacity of two, so a filled outer shell, not an octet, is what justifies placing helium in group 18, making D correct.
(Choice A) Although the \(1s^2\) configuration does end in an \(s^2\) subshell, the alkaline earth metals of group 2 have an \(ns^2\) outer subshell that sits outside an already-filled inner shell, so those two \(s\) electrons are loosely held valence electrons readily lost to form \(2+\) ions; in helium the \(1s^2\) pair is the entire filled \(n=1\) shell with no inner core, producing inertness rather than the reactivity of group 2.
(Choice B) Helium has only two electrons in total, so it cannot carry eight valence electrons; the heavier noble gases reach an octet (\(ns^2 np^6\)) only from the second period onward, whereas helium achieves a filled outer shell with a duet, and treating its stability as an octet misstates its electron count.
(Choice C) The placement is not arbitrary convention: helium is positioned in group 18 precisely because its filled \(n=1\) shell gives it the closed-shell electronic structure and chemical inertness that define the noble-gas group, a clear electronic basis rather than historical accident.
Skill 1$ex48$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc48$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc48$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a48A$Assigns helium to group 2 from the surface s^2 ending, ignoring that the 1s^2 pair is a filled shell rather than reactive ns^2 valence electrons$a48A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a48B$Claims helium has eight valence electrons like the other noble gases, a factual error since helium has only two electrons and reaches stability via a duet$a48B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a48C$Dismisses the placement as historical convention with no electronic basis, denying the filled n=1 duet that actually justifies grouping helium with the noble gases$a48C$ FROM q;

-- Q9 [Chemistry of the Groups · medium · Skill 1 · ans A] (g49)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt49$Manganese forms stable compounds spanning \(\text{Mn}^{2+}\) through \(\text{Mn}^{7+}\), whereas calcium, its near neighbor in the same period, is confined to \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\). The wider range available to manganese is most directly attributable to which feature of its valence electronic structure?$qt49$,
    $op49$[{"label":"A","text":"The \\(3d\\) and \\(4s\\) electrons lie close enough in energy that successive ionizations remove them at comparable cost."},{"label":"B","text":"The \\(3d\\) subshell is completely filled, so its electrons are equally available for removal one at a time."},{"label":"C","text":"The \\(4s\\) electrons are far higher in energy than the \\(3d\\) electrons, so only the \\(4s\\) pair is ever lost."},{"label":"D","text":"Partially filled \\(3d\\) orbitals lower the first ionization energy enough to strip electrons in a single step."}]$op49$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex49$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. Transition metals such as \(\text{Mn}\) (\([\text{Ar}]3d^54s^2\)) show variable oxidation states because the \(3d\) and \(4s\) valence levels are very similar in energy. After the two \(4s\) electrons are removed (giving \(\text{Mn}^{2+}\)), the \(3d\) electrons can be removed in successive, energetically comparable steps without an abrupt jump in ionization energy, so a continuous ladder of stable states up to \(\text{Mn}^{7+}\) (loss of all seven valence electrons) becomes accessible. A representative main-group metal like \(\text{Ca}\) (\([\text{Ar}]4s^2\)) has only the two \(4s\) electrons outside a noble-gas core; removing a third electron would breach that core at enormous energetic cost, locking it into \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\). (Choice B) The \(3d\) subshell in \(\text{Mn}\) is half-filled (\(3d^5\)), not completely filled, and a filled subshell would be more stable and less prone to losing electrons, which is the opposite of what produces a broad oxidation-state range. (Choice C) The \(4s\) and \(3d\) levels are actually close in energy, not widely separated; if only the \(4s\) pair could ever be lost, the element would be restricted to a single \(+2\) state rather than the observed range. (Choice D) A low first ionization energy governs only the removal of the first electron and does not strip multiple electrons simultaneously; oxidation states arise from sequential, single-electron ionizations, and high oxidation states still require successively larger ionization energies. Skill 1$ex49$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc49$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc49$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
