UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a49C$Reverses the true near-degeneracy of 3d and 4s by asserting a large energy gap, leading to a single fixed oxidation state.$a49C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a49D$Conflates a single first-ionization event with the sequential multi-step ionizations that actually generate variable oxidation states.$a49D$ FROM q;

-- Q10 [Chemistry of the Groups · medium · Skill 1 · ans B] (g50)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt50$On the periodic table, \(\text{H}\) is printed at the head of group 1 above the alkali metals, yet it is classified as a nonmetal. Which feature best accounts for its placement there while still sharing character with group 17?$qt50$,
    $op50$[{"label":"A","text":"Its \\(1s^1\\) configuration mirrors the \\(ns^1\\) valence of the alkali metals, so \\(\\text{H}\\) is itself a reactive metal that, like \\(\\text{Cl}_2\\), happens to exist as a diatomic gas."},{"label":"B","text":"Its lone \\(1s^1\\) electron parallels the \\(ns^1\\) valence of the alkali metals, yet as a nonmetal it can also gain one electron to form \\(\\text{H}^-\\) and complete its shell as the group 17 halogens do."},{"label":"C","text":"It is positioned in group 1 because, like the halogens, it is one electron short of a filled shell, and this shared deficiency makes \\(\\text{H}\\) behave chiefly as a metal."},{"label":"D","text":"It sits above group 1 solely because it readily forms the \\(\\text{H}^+\\) cation as the alkali metals do, and it otherwise shares no chemical character with the group 17 halogens."}]$op50$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex50$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. Hydrogen is placed at the top of group 1 because its ground-state configuration is \(1s^1\), a single valence electron that parallels the \(ns^1\) valence of the alkali metals, and like them it can lose that electron to form \(\text{H}^+\). However, \(\text{H}\) is a nonmetal: it is a diatomic gas, \(\text{H}_2\), with no metallic character. It also resembles the group 17 halogens because it is one electron short of the helium configuration and can gain an electron to form the hydride ion, \(\text{H}^-\), completing its \(1s\) shell. This dual resemblance, plus its nonmetallic nature, is exactly why its placement is ambiguous, making B correct. (Choice A) The premise correctly notes the \(1s^1 / ns^1\) parallel, but \(\text{H}\) is a nonmetal, not a metal; existing as the diatomic gas \(\text{H}_2\) reflects nonmetallic, not metallic, behavior. (Choice C) Hydrogen occupies the group 1 position, not a placement justified by being one electron short of a filled shell, and it is a nonmetal rather than chiefly metallic; the metal/nonmetal assignment here is inverted. (Choice D) Forming \(\text{H}^+\) like the alkali metals is a genuine trait, but it is not the sole basis for the placement, and \(\text{H}\) does share group 17 character, since it can also gain an electron to form \(\text{H}^-\) like the halogens. Skill 1$ex50$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc50$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc50$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a50A$group-1 position implies hydrogen is a metal$a50A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a50C$halogen-like deficiency cited as group-1 placement reason with metal/nonmetal inversion$a50C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a50D$H+ formation true but sole-cause overreach denying halogen resemblance$a50D$ FROM q;

-- Q11 [Chemistry of the Groups · medium · Skill 2 · ans C] (g51)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt51$A pea-sized piece of potassium is dropped onto water containing a few drops of phenolphthalein. The metal skates across the surface, a gas ignites with a lilac flame, and the liquid turns pink. The igniting gas and the species responsible for the color change are most consistent with$qt51$,
    $op51$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{O}_2\\) liberated from the water, with dissolved \\(\\text{K}^+\\) raising the pH"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{H}_2\\) liberated from the water, with \\(\\text{K}_2\\text{O}\\) dissolving to lower the pH"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{H}_2\\) liberated from the water, with \\(\\text{OH}^-\\) from dissolved \\(\\text{KOH}\\) raising the pH"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{H}_2\\) liberated from the metal, with \\(\\text{K}^+\\) hydrolyzing to lower the pH"}]$op51$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex51$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.' Alkali metals such as potassium react vigorously with water according to \(2\text{K} + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow 2\text{KOH} + \text{H}_2\). The metal reduces hydrogen from the \(+1\) state in water to \(\text{H}_2\) gas, which is the flammable gas that ignites; the heat of the exothermic reaction sets it alight. The other product, \(\text{KOH}\), is a soluble strong base that dissociates into \(\text{K}^+\) and \(\text{OH}^-\). The excess \(\text{OH}^-\) raises the pH, turning phenolphthalein pink. The lilac flame is the characteristic emission color of potassium. (Choice A) Water supplies its hydrogen, not its oxygen, to the redox reaction; \(\text{O}_2\) is not evolved, and \(\text{K}^+\) is the conjugate of a strong base, so it is pH-neutral and cannot raise the pH on its own. (Choice B) The gas identity is correct, but \(\text{K}_2\text{O}\) is not the dissolved product, and any basic oxide that did form would react with water to give \(\text{KOH}\), raising the pH rather than lowering it. (Choice D) The hydrogen in \(\text{H}_2\) originates from the \(\text{O}-\text{H}\) bonds of water being reduced, not from the metal, and \(\text{K}^+\) does not hydrolyze appreciably because it is the cation of a strong base, so the solution becomes basic, not acidic. Skill 2$ex51$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc51$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc51$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a51A$believing the liberated gas is oxygen and that the spectator alkali cation itself makes the solution basic$a51A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a51B$treating a basic metal oxide as a pH-lowering (acidic) species$a51B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a51D$sourcing the H2 from the metal rather than from reduction of water's hydrogen, and invoking cation hydrolysis to acidify$a51D$ FROM q;

-- Q12 [Chemistry of the Groups · medium · Skill 2 · ans D] (g52)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt52$A stockroom technician is restocking sealed sample vials of the four common halogens and needs to label which one will appear as a freely pouring liquid at the room temperature of the lab (about \(25\,^\circ\text{C}\)), as opposed to a gas above its sample or a solid that must be scooped. Given that dispersion forces between diatomic halogen molecules grow stronger as molar mass increases down group 17, which sample is most likely to be found in the liquid state under these conditions?$qt52$,
    $op52$[{"label":"A","text":"Fluorine, \\(\\text{F}_2\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorine, \\(\\text{Cl}_2\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"Iodine, \\(\\text{I}_2\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"Bromine, \\(\\text{Br}_2\\)"}]$op52$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex52$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.' Down group 17 the halogens gain electrons and grow in molar mass, so the dispersion (London) forces between the nonpolar diatomic molecules strengthen progressively. This rising intermolecular attraction raises the boiling and melting points down the group, producing a smooth transition in physical state: fluorine and chlorine are gases at \(25\,^\circ\text{C}\), bromine is a liquid (boiling point about \(59\,^\circ\text{C}\), melting point about \(-7\,^\circ\text{C}\)), and iodine is a solid. Bromine sits in the narrow window where its dispersion forces are strong enough to keep the substance condensed yet still weak enough that it does not solidify, so \(\text{Br}_2\) is the halogen found as a liquid at room temperature. (Choice A) Fluorine is the lightest halogen with the weakest dispersion forces and the lowest boiling point (about \(-188\,^\circ\text{C}\)); at room temperature it exists as a pale yellow gas, not a liquid. (Choice B) Chlorine is heavier than fluorine but its dispersion forces are still too weak to condense at \(25\,^\circ\text{C}\) (boiling point about \(-34\,^\circ\text{C}\)), so it remains a greenish-yellow gas. (Choice C) Iodine has the strongest dispersion forces among these halogens because of its large, highly polarizable electron cloud, raising its melting point above room temperature (about \(114\,^\circ\text{C}\)); it is therefore a solid that sublimes rather than a liquid at \(25\,^\circ\text{C}\). Skill 2$ex52$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc52$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc52$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a52A$Assumes the lightest halogen with the weakest forces is the one that condenses to a liquid$a52A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a52B$Names a halogen that is a gas at room temperature, confusing it with the liquid member$a52B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a52C$Treats the halogen with the strongest dispersion forces as the liquid rather than recognizing it is a solid$a52C$ FROM q;

-- Q13 [Chemistry of the Groups · medium · Skill 1 · ans A] (g53)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt53$A technician characterizing a sealed sample of pure \(\text{Ar}\) records that the substance is a gas at room temperature and condenses to a liquid only when chilled to about \(-186\,^\circ\text{C}\). Treating this behavior as representative of group 18, which statement most likely accounts for the gaseous state and very low boiling point shared across the noble gases?$qt53$,
    $op53$[{"label":"A","text":"They exist as individual, monatomic atoms held to one another only by weak interatomic attractions, so very little energy is needed to separate them into the gas phase."},{"label":"B","text":"They exist as monatomic atoms bound to one another by unusually strong interatomic attractions, which raises their boiling points well above room temperature."},{"label":"C","text":"They exist as diatomic molecules such as \\(\\text{Ar}_2\\), and breaking these molecular units apart upon vaporization requires only a small amount of energy."},{"label":"D","text":"They are chemically inert because each atom already holds a filled valence shell, and this lack of reactivity is what keeps them in the gas phase at room temperature."}]$op53$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex53$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.' The noble gases of group 18 exist as single, monatomic atoms rather than as bonded molecules, and the only attractions acting between these separate atoms are weak interatomic (dispersion) forces. Because so little energy is required to overcome such weak attractions, the noble gases are gases at room temperature and have very low boiling points, exactly as the \(-186\,^\circ\text{C}\) condensation point of \(\text{Ar}\) illustrates; this makes A correct. (Choice B) The interatomic attractions between noble-gas atoms are weak, not unusually strong, and weak attractions produce low boiling points; strong attractions would instead resist separation and raise the boiling point, which is the opposite of the observed behavior. (Choice C) The noble gases occur as individual monatomic atoms, not as diatomic molecules like \(\text{Ar}_2\); diatomic units characterize elements such as the group 17 halogens, so describing the vaporizing species as a molecule misrepresents the physical form of group 18. (Choice D) A filled valence shell does correctly describe noble-gas electron configuration, but that property governs their chemical inertness, not their physical state; the gaseous nature and low boiling point arise from the weak attractions between monatomic atoms, so attributing the physical state to chemical unreactivity addresses the wrong property. Skill 1$ex53$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc53$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc53$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a53B$strong-attractions-give-low-boiling-point sign inversion$a53B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a53C$noble gases treated as diatomic molecules like halogens$a53C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a53D$physical state attributed to chemical inertness rather than weak interatomic forces$a53D$ FROM q;

-- Q14 [Chemistry of the Groups · medium · Skill 2 · ans B] (g54)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt54$Selenium lies directly below sulfur in group 16. When selenium reacts with a strongly electropositive metal such as rubidium, the selenium atom most likely reaches the stable configuration of which species?$qt54$,
    $op54$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{As}\\), by losing a single electron from its highest-energy orbital"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Kr}\\), by gaining two electrons to complete its valence shell"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Br}\\), by gaining one electron to half-fill its \\(p\\) subshell"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Ge}\\), by losing two electrons to empty its \\(p\\) subshell"}]$op54$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex54$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. Selenium is a chalcogen (group 16) with a valence configuration of \(4s^2 4p^4\), so it is two electrons short of the next noble gas. When paired with a strongly electropositive metal like \(\text{Rb}\), \(\text{Se}\) gains two electrons to form the selenide ion \(\text{Se}^{2-}\), which is isoelectronic with krypton \((4s^2 4p^6)\) and completes the valence octet. This is the same -2 behavior seen across the oxygen group, where the large electronegativity gap with an active metal drives full electron transfer rather than partial filling. (Choice A) Reaching the configuration of \(\text{As}\) would require losing one electron, but a chalcogen reacting with an electropositive metal gains electrons to fill its shell, not loses them toward the preceding group-15 configuration. (Choice C) Adding only one electron gives the configuration of \(\text{Br}\) and leaves the \(p\) subshell with five electrons, one short of the closed-shell octet that drives the favored -2 charge; the halogen-like single-electron gain does not apply to a group-16 atom. (Choice D) Emptying the \(p\) subshell to match \(\text{Ge}\) would mean losing the four \(p\) electrons, which is energetically prohibitive for a relatively electronegative chalcogen bonded to an active metal that itself donates electrons. Skill 2$ex54$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc54$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc54$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a54A$Treating the chalcogen as an electron donor (losing one electron toward the group-15 configuration) rather than an acceptor$a54A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a54C$Applying the halogen single-electron-gain rule to a chalcogen, reaching a -1 / group-17 configuration$a54C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a54D$Believing the atom empties its p subshell to a pseudo-noble-gas core, forming a +4 cation instead of the -2 anion$a54D$ FROM q;

-- Q15 [Chemistry of the Groups · medium · Skill 2 · ans C] (g55)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt55$A chemist adds a small amount of bromine (\(\text{Br}_2\)) to an aqueous solution that contains both chloride ions (\(\text{Cl}^-\)) and iodide ions (\(\text{I}^-\)). Assuming the only redox reactions that occur are between a halogen and a halide, which species in the mixture is most likely to be oxidized to its elemental form by the added bromine?$qt55$,
    $op55$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Cl}^-\\), because chlorine sits above bromine in group 17 and the lighter halide is always displaced first"},{"label":"B","text":"Neither halide, because bromine is a weaker oxidizer than both chlorine and iodine and cannot oxidize either ion"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{I}^-\\), because oxidizing strength decreases down group 17, so bromine is a stronger oxidizer than iodine and can pull an electron from iodide"},{"label":"D","text":"Both \\(\\text{Cl}^-\\) and \\(\\text{I}^-\\), because bromine lies between them in the group and oxidizes the halide on either side of it"}]$op55$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex55$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.' Oxidizing strength and reactivity among the group 17 halogens decrease moving down the group, because the lighter (smaller) halogens hold their valence shell closer to the nucleus and attract and gain an electron more readily. The descending order of oxidizing strength is therefore \(\text{F}_2 > \text{Cl}_2 > \text{Br}_2 > \text{I}_2\). A given halogen (\(\text{X}_2\)) can oxidize the halide ion of any element below it in the group but not above it. Bromine is a stronger oxidizer than iodine, so \(\text{Br}_2\) can remove an electron from \(\text{I}^-\), oxidizing it to \(\text{I}_2\) (and being reduced to \(\text{Br}^-\)). Bromine is a weaker oxidizer than chlorine, so it cannot oxidize \(\text{Cl}^-\). Thus only iodide is oxidized to its elemental form. (Choice A) Chlorine does sit above bromine in group 17, but position above means chlorine is the stronger oxidizer; a halogen cannot oxidize the halide of an element above it, so bromine leaves \(\text{Cl}^-\) untouched rather than displacing it first. (Choice B) Bromine is indeed weaker than chlorine, but oxidizing strength decreases down the group, making bromine stronger than iodine, not weaker; bromine therefore can oxidize iodide. (Choice D) A halogen oxidizes only the halides of elements lower in the group, not those on both sides; bromine oxidizes \(\text{I}^-\) (below it) but not \(\text{Cl}^-\) (above it), so the two halides are not equivalent here. Skill 2$ex55$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc55$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc55$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a55A$Treats the lighter, higher-positioned halogen as the one that gets displaced/oxidized, inverting the rule that a halogen oxidizes only halides below it$a55A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a55B$Correctly notes bromine is weaker than chlorine but wrongly extends weakness to the bromine-iodine comparison, concluding no reaction$a55B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a55D$Assumes a halogen oxidizes the halides on both sides of its group position rather than only those below it$a55D$ FROM q;

-- Q16 [Chemistry of the Groups · medium · Skill 2 · ans D] (g56)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt56$A materials engineer choosing a filament metal notes that tungsten, \(\text{W}\) (\([\text{Xe}]4f^{14}5d^{4}6s^{2}\)), stays solid in a glowing lamp where potassium, \(\text{K}\) (\([\text{Ar}]4s^{1}\)), would have long since melted into a puddle. Assuming both are held by metallic bonding, which metal is expected to have the higher melting point, and what most likely accounts for the difference?$qt56$,
    $op56$[{"label":"A","text":"Potassium, because its single \\(4s\\) valence electron is more loosely held and its larger atomic radius lets the metal cations pack into a stronger, lower-energy lattice."},{"label":"B","text":"Neither differs meaningfully, because both are metals whose atoms release valence electrons into a shared electron sea, so their lattices require comparable energy to break apart."},{"label":"C","text":"Tungsten, because its accessible range of oxidation states lets it form additional ionic linkages within the solid that reinforce the lattice beyond ordinary metallic bonding."},{"label":"D","text":"Tungsten, because both its \\(5d\\) and \\(6s\\) electrons enter the delocalized electron sea, contributing more bonding electrons per atom and thus stronger metallic bonding than potassium's lone \\(4s\\) electron."}]$op56$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex56$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. Metallic bonding strength scales with the number of delocalized electrons each atom donates to the electron sea and the resulting strength and number of metal-metal bonds. Potassium, a group-1 alkali metal, contributes only its single \(4s\) electron, giving a sparse electron sea, weak bonding, and a low melting point (\(\text{K}\) melts near \(63\,^\circ\text{C}\)). Tungsten is a \(d\)-block transition metal whose \((n-1)d\) electrons (the \(5d\)) participate in metal-metal bonding alongside the \(6s\) electrons, so many more electrons are delocalized; the resulting metallic bonding is far stronger, and \(\text{W}\) has the highest melting point of any metal (about \(3422\,^\circ\text{C}\)). Therefore tungsten melts at a far higher temperature, and the cause is the larger pool of bonding electrons from both \(5d\) and \(6s\) participation. (Choice A) This reverses the actual trend: potassium's loosely held single valence electron and large radius give weaker, more diffuse metallic bonding and a lower melting point, not a stronger lattice. A bigger atomic radius and lower ionization energy correlate with weaker metallic bonding among these metals, not stronger. (Choice B) Sharing an electron sea is common to all metals, but the quantity of delocalized electrons differs sharply; treating all metals as bonding equally ignores that transition metals contribute many more bonding electrons, which is why their melting points greatly exceed those of the alkali metals. (Choice C) The higher melting point is correctly assigned to tungsten, but the cause is wrong: the elevated melting point arises from stronger metallic (not ionic) bonding in the elemental solid, and variable oxidation states do not introduce ionic linkages that hold the pure metal together. Skill 2$ex56$,
    'medium', '4E', $cc56$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc56$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a56A$Larger atomic radius / lower ionization energy of the alkali metal wrongly taken to give stronger metallic bonding and a higher melting point$a56A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a56B$All metals bond alike because they all have an electron sea, ignoring differences in number of delocalized electrons$a56B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a56C$Correct metal identified but melting point attributed to ionic bonding / variable oxidation states rather than metallic bonding strength$a56C$ FROM q;

-- Q17 [Chemistry of the Groups · hard · Skill 2 · ans A] (g57)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt57$A lab technician needs a group-2 metal that will displace hydrogen from cold liquid water at the fastest observable rate during a demonstration. Working only from each element's position in the periodic table, the technician must choose among magnesium, calcium, strontium, and barium. Which metal should be selected for the demonstration?$qt57$,
    $op57$[{"label":"A","text":"Barium"},{"label":"B","text":"Magnesium"},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium"},{"label":"D","text":"Strontium"}]$op57$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex57$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. Within group 2 (the alkaline earth metals), reactivity toward water increases moving down the group. Descending the column, the valence \(ns^2\) electrons occupy shells of higher principal quantum number and are increasingly shielded from the nuclear charge, so they are held less tightly and are lost more readily. Loss of those two electrons to form the \(M^{2+}\) ion is the step that governs reaction with water, so the lowest-lying metal in the set reacts most vigorously. Among magnesium, calcium, strontium, and barium, barium is lowest in the group and therefore reacts fastest with cold water, making it the correct selection. (Choice B) Magnesium is the highest member of this set; its valence electrons are closest to the nucleus and most strongly held, so it reacts only very slowly with cold water and most slowly of the four. (Choice C) Calcium lies above strontium and barium, so its reaction with cold water, while faster than magnesium's, is less vigorous than that of the heavier members. (Choice D) Strontium reacts more vigorously than calcium but lies one period above barium, so its valence electrons remain somewhat more tightly bound and its reaction is not the fastest in the set. Skill 2$ex57$,
    'hard', '4E', $cc57$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc57$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a57B$Treats reactivity as increasing UP the group, selecting the topmost (least reactive) metal$a57B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a57C$Chooses a metal that reacts more than magnesium but is not the lowest in the group$a57C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a57D$Chooses the second-lowest metal, recognizing the down-group trend but stopping one period short of barium$a57D$ FROM q;

-- Q18 [Chemistry of the Groups · hard · Skill 2 · ans B] (g58)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt58$A sample of molten potassium is exposed to bromine vapor, and the metal is consumed completely. Assuming each element reaches the charge favored by its group's electronic structure, what is the formula of the binary salt that forms?$qt58$,
    $op58$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{K}_2\\text{Br}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{KBr}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{KBr}_2\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{K}_2\\text{Br}_3\\)"}]$op58$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex58$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. Potassium sits in Group 1, whose atoms have a single \(ns^1\) valence electron; losing it gives the noble-gas configuration of argon and the \(\text{K}^+\) cation. Bromine sits in Group 17, whose atoms have an \(ns^2 np^5\) valence shell; gaining one electron completes the octet and gives the \(\text{Br}^-\) anion. A neutral formula unit pairs one \(+1\) charge with one \(-1\) charge, so the ratio is 1:1 and the salt is \(\text{KBr}\). (Choice A) \(\text{K}_2\text{Br}\) would require bromine to carry a \(-2\) charge, but a Group 17 atom needs only one electron to reach a noble-gas configuration and does not form a \(-2\) ion. (Choice C) \(\text{KBr}_2\) would require potassium to carry a \(+2\) charge; removing a second electron from \(\text{K}^+\) breaks the stable argon core and is energetically prohibitive, so potassium remains \(+1\). (Choice D) \(\text{K}_2\text{Br}_3\) corresponds to a \(+3/-2\) charge pairing, neither of which matches the \(+1\) and \(-1\) ions dictated by the Group 1 and Group 17 electronic structures. Skill 2$ex58$,
    'hard', '4E', $cc58$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc58$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
