-- Q19 [Chemistry of the Groups · hard · Skill 4 · ans C] (g59)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt59$A freshly cut sample of a silvery metal is soft enough to slice with a knife and floats briefly on water before reacting. The reaction is vigorous, evolves a flammable gas, and leaves a solution that turns red litmus blue. When the data are reduced, exactly \(0.5\ \text{mol}\) of \(\text{H}_2\) is released per mole of metal consumed. Based on these observations, the element most likely belongs to which group of the periodic table?$qt59$,
    $op59$[{"label":"A","text":"Group 2, the alkaline earth metals, since they also react with water to give a basic solution and hydrogen gas"},{"label":"B","text":"Group 17, the halogens, since the highly reactive nonmetal produces an acidic solution on contact with water"},{"label":"C","text":"Group 1, the alkali metals, whose \\(+1\\) cations give a \\(1{:}0.5\\) metal-to-\\(\\text{H}_2\\) ratio and strongly basic hydroxides"},{"label":"D","text":"Group 14, the carbon group, whose softer members form covalent hydrides while displacing hydrogen from water"}]$op59$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex59$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.' The combined evidence points to the alkali metals. Group 1 elements have a single valence electron (\(ns^1\)), are soft enough to cut with a knife, have densities low enough that several float on water, and react vigorously with cold water: \(2\,\text{M} + 2\,\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow 2\,\text{MOH} + \text{H}_2\). Because each metal atom loses one electron to form a \(+1\) cation, two moles of metal are required to release one mole of \(\text{H}_2\), giving exactly the observed \(0.5\ \text{mol}\) of \(\text{H}_2\) per mole of metal. The product \(\text{MOH}\) is a strong base, which accounts for the litmus turning blue. The \(1{:}0.5\) stoichiometry is the decisive constraint, since it fixes the cation charge at \(+1\). (Choice A) The alkaline earth metals do react with water to give a basic solution and \(\text{H}_2\), but they form \(+2\) cations: \(\text{M} + 2\,\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{M(OH)}_2 + \text{H}_2\) releases one mole of \(\text{H}_2\) per mole of metal, twice the observed amount, and these metals are also harder and denser than the sample described. (Choice B) The halogens are nonmetals that are not cut with a knife and do not liberate \(\text{H}_2\) by displacement; their reactions with water and their hydrogen halides such as \(\text{HCl}\) yield acidic solutions, which would turn litmus red rather than blue, reversing both the metal-versus-nonmetal character and the acid-versus-base outcome. (Choice C) keyed correct. (Choice D) The carbon-group elements form predominantly covalent compounds and do not react with cold water to evolve hydrogen gas while generating a strongly basic solution, so the reactive, base-forming behavior observed here is inconsistent with Group 14. Skill 4$ex59$,
    'hard', '4E', $cc59$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc59$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
