-- Q20 [Chemistry of the Groups · hard · Skill 4 · ans D] (g60)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'The Periodic Table', 'Chemistry of the Groups',
    $qt60$A chemist exposes freshly cut samples of \(\text{Na}\) and \(\text{Mg}\), both period 3 metals, to moist air and finds that the \(\text{Na}\) sample tarnishes far more rapidly. Using periodic-trend reasoning, how do the two metals compare in reactivity, and what charge does each adopt in its resulting ion?$qt60$,
    $op60$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Mg}\\) is the more reactive metal because it can release two valence electrons, forming \\(\\text{Mg}^{2+}\\), whereas \\(\\text{Na}\\) forms only \\(\\text{Na}^+\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"The two metals are comparably reactive because they share the same period, with \\(\\text{Na}\\) forming \\(\\text{Na}^+\\) and \\(\\text{Mg}\\) forming \\(\\text{Mg}^+\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Na}\\) is the more reactive metal, and because it gives up its electron so readily it forms \\(\\text{Na}^{2+}\\), while the less reactive \\(\\text{Mg}\\) forms \\(\\text{Mg}^+\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Na}\\) is the more reactive metal yet forms the lower-charge \\(\\text{Na}^+\\), while the less reactive \\(\\text{Mg}\\) forms \\(\\text{Mg}^{2+}\\)"}]$op60$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex60$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.' Across a period, first ionization energy rises left to right, so the Group 1 alkali metal \(\text{Na}\) (\([\text{Ne}]3s^1\)) loses its single valence electron more easily than the Group 2 alkaline earth metal \(\text{Mg}\) (\([\text{Ne}]3s^2\)); \(\text{Na}\) is therefore the more reactive metal, consistent with its faster tarnishing. Reactivity here is governed by how readily the first electron leaves, not by how many electrons the atom can ultimately lose. The ion charge is set independently by the valence configuration: \(\text{Na}\) empties its \(3s^1\) to reach the noble-gas core and stops at \(\text{Na}^+\), while \(\text{Mg}\) loses both \(3s^2\) electrons to reach the same core as \(\text{Mg}^{2+}\). Thus the more reactive metal forms the lower-charge ion. (Choice A reverses the reactivity relationship: forming a higher-charge ion does not make a metal more reactive, and \(\text{Mg}\)'s higher first ionization energy makes it the less reactive of the two. Choice B ignores the differing valence configurations; \(\text{Mg}\) cannot stop at \(\text{Mg}^+\) because removing both \(3s\) electrons is needed to reach the stable \([\text{Ne}]\) core, and the period match does not equalize first ionization energies, which still rise toward \(\text{Mg}\).) (Choice C correctly identifies \(\text{Na}\) as more reactive but then ties ion charge to reactivity; ease of losing the first electron does not let \(\text{Na}\) shed a second core electron, so it stops at \(\text{Na}^+\), and \(\text{Mg}\) reaches \(\text{Mg}^{2+}\), not \(\text{Mg}^+\).) Skill 4$ex60$,
    'hard', '4E', $cc60$The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure$cc60$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
